.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-REDRIVE-DLQ 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-redrive-dlq \- move messages out of a dead letter queue and back where they failed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eqs redrive-dlq
.BR \-\-queue | \-q
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-target\-queue | \-t
.IR NAME ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Moves every message out of a dead letter queue and back into the queue
it failed in.
.PP
A message reaches a dead letter queue by being received more times than
its source queue's
.I maxReceiveCount
allows \- it was delivered, not processed, and delivered again, until
euclid stopped offering it. The dead letter queue is where those land so
somebody can look at them. Putting them back, once whatever rejected
them is fixed, is the other half of that and is what this does.
.SH WHAT MAKES A QUEUE A DEAD LETTER QUEUE
Nothing on the queue itself. The relationship is only ever written the
other way round \- a queue names the queue its failures go to \- so a
dead letter queue is simply one that some other queue points at.
.PP
Naming a queue nothing points at is refused:
.PP
.RS
.nf
error: redrive-dlq failed (HTTP 400): Queue is not a dead letter queue,
ern: ... - no queue names it as its dead letter queue
.fi
.RE
.PP
which is deliberate. An ordinary queue has no "original queue" to return
anything to, and moving its messages somewhere chosen by the gateway
would be worse than refusing.
.SH WHERE THE MESSAGES GO
Where one queue feeds the dead letter queue \- the usual arrangement \-
everything goes back to it and
.B \-\-target\-queue
is unnecessary.
.PP
Where several do, "the original queue" is only answerable per message,
so each goes back to the queue it actually came from. euclid records
that on the message as it moves it, so the answer is exact rather than
guessed.
.PP
Messages that predate euclid recording it have no origin. They are left
in the dead letter queue rather than placed arbitrarily, and counted in
.I remaining
so it is clear something was left:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "ern": "...:queue:shared-dlq",
  "messages": 42,
  "remaining": 3,
  "targets": [ { "queueErn": "...:queue:orders", "messages": 42 } ],
  "note": "Messages remain in the dead letter queue because no source
           queue is recorded for them. Name one with a target queue to
           move them."
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-target\-queue
is how to place those deliberately. It has to be one of the queues that
actually feed this dead letter queue \- anything else would be a move
rather than a redrive, and would put messages somewhere they were never
sent.
.SH WHAT A REDRIVEN MESSAGE LOOKS LIKE
It starts over as though it were new: available immediately, with no
receipt handle from whatever last held it, and its receive count back to
zero. That last part matters \- a redriven message gets the source
queue's full allowance again, rather than returning one failure away
from being dead a second time.
.PP
Fix what made them fail before running this. Messages redriven into a
consumer that still rejects them simply come back, and the second round
trip tells nobody anything the first did not.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fINAME\fR
Dead letter queue to empty. Required. A queue name is enough; a full ERN
also works and is what reaches another namespace.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target\-queue " " \fINAME\fR
Queue to move the messages into. Only needed when several queues share
this dead letter queue, or to place messages with no recorded origin.
Must be a queue that uses this dead letter queue.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The messages were moved. Counts are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing arguments, no session, the named queue does not exist, it is not
a dead letter queue, the target queue does not use it, or the server
rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Put everything back after fixing the consumer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eqs redrive-dlq --queue orders-dlq
.fi
.RE
.PP
Place the messages that have no recorded origin:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eqs redrive-dlq --queue shared-dlq --target-queue orders
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-create-queue (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-list-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-purge-queue (1)
